\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Theorem}
\title{Eliminating a positive Gaussian block}
\date{2026-10-17}

\p{Let #{E=X\mathbin\perp Y} be a specified orthogonal sum of
finite-dimensional real inner-product spaces. Write #{h_E,h_X,h_Y}
for their pairings, #{i_X,i_Y} for inclusion and #{p_X,p_Y} for
orthogonal projection. The addition isometry is
#{J(x,y)=i_Xx+i_Yy}. Use \vocabk{canonical Euclidean volume}{fdip-002M} on
uncompleted Borel sets. Then [product volume](fdip-002Q) gives
#{J_*(\lambda_X\otimes\lambda_Y)=\lambda_E}.
Zero summands are allowed; their volume is unit mass on a singleton.}
\p{Let #{Q:E\to E} be a self-adjoint
\vocabk{positive precision parameter}{fdip-0004}. Its
\vocabk{components for this splitting}{fdip-001T} are
##{\begin{aligned}
A&=p_XQi_X:X\to X,\\
B&=p_XQi_Y:Y\to X,\\
C&=p_YQi_Y:Y\to Y.
\end{aligned}}
The fourth component is #{p_YQi_X=B^\dagger}, where the adjoint
uses the specified pairings. Both #{C} and the
\newvocab{effective precision}, or real \newvocab{Schur complement},
##{S=A-BC^{-1}B^\dagger:X\to X}
are positive definite and invertible. This is the real positive
specialization of the \vocabk{split Schur map}{fdip-001T}.}
\p{Put #{w_Q(z)=\exp(-h_E(z,Qz)/2)} and #{q=\dim Y}.
For every #{x\in X}, the exact fiber integral is
##{\begin{aligned}
I_Q(x)&=\int_Y w_Q(J(x,y))\,d\lambda_Y(y)\\
 &=c_C\exp(-h_X(x,Sx)/2),\\
c_C&=\frac{(2\pi)^{q/2}}{\sqrt{\det C}}=Z_C>0.
\end{aligned}}
Moreover #{\det Q=\det C\det S}. Thus elimination induces both
#{S} and a positive finite scalar #{c_C}.}

\proof{
\p{[Positivity under elimination](fdip-001Y) applies to these real
inner products. Explicitly, testing #{Q} on #{i_Yy} gives #{C>0};
finite dimension makes #{C} invertible. Put #{K=C^{-1}B^\dagger}.
The [orthogonal congruence identity](fdip-001X) is
##{\begin{aligned}
&h_E(J(x,y),QJ(x,y))\\
&\quad=h_X(x,Sx)\\
&\qquad+h_Y(y+Kx,C(y+Kx)).
\end{aligned}}
Indeed #{CK=B^\dagger} and #{K^\dagger=BC^{-1}}; expansion cancels
the term subtracted in #{S}. The self-adjoint inverse of #{C} makes
#{S} self-adjoint. For #{x\ne0}, testing on #{J(x,-Kx)\ne0} gives
#{h_X(x,Sx)>0}.}
\p{For fixed #{x}, translation by #{Kx} preserves
[reference volume](fdip-002K). Square completion and
[positive Gaussian normalization](fdip-002T) therefore give
##{\begin{aligned}
I_Q(x)&=e^{-h_X(x,Sx)/2}
 \int_Y e^{-h_Y(v,Cv)/2}\,d\lambda_Y(v)\\
 &=e^{-h_X(x,Sx)/2}Z_C.
\end{aligned}}
This is a nonnegative integral with a positive finite prefactor;
no signed Fubini interchange is needed. The same square completion
shows that the stationary field is #{y_*(x)=-Kx}. Minimization alone
does not evaluate the fiber integral or its scalar.}
\p{For the determinant, let #{V(x,y)=(x,y+Kx)} and
#{D(x,y)=(Sx,Cy)} on #{X\oplus Y}. Congruence gives
#{J^{-1}QJ=V^\dagger DV}. Choose bases #{e_1,\ldots,e_p} of #{X}
and #{f_1,\ldots,f_q} of #{Y}, with the same ordered concatenation
in domain and codomain. In the top wedge of the images under #{V},
we have #{Ve_j=e_j+Ke_j} and #{Vf_k=f_k}. Any term choosing some
#{Ke_j} has more than #{q} vectors in #{Y}, so it vanishes. The
remaining term is the original top wedge; hence #{\det V=1}.
Now choose orthonormal bases of #{X} and #{Y}, independently of
the arbitrary bases used for the wedge calculation. Their concatenation
is orthonormal on #{X\oplus Y}. In these coordinates the adjoint
is the transpose and has the same determinant, by reindexing the
determinant permutation sum.
The direct sum #{D} acts on the top wedge by #{\det S\det C}.
Multiplicativity of this scalar and invariance under conjugation
now prove #{\det Q=\det C\det S}. Empty bases give determinant one,
so the argument includes zero summands.}
}

\p{Taking minus the logarithm of the positive fiber integral defines
an exact \newvocab{effective action}:
##{\begin{aligned}
\mathcal A_{\mathrm{eff}}(x)
 &=\tfrac12h_X(x,Sx)-\log c_C\\
 &=\tfrac12h_X(x,Sx)+\tfrac12\log\det C\\
 &\quad-\tfrac q2\log(2\pi).
\end{aligned}}
The constant cancels from normalized retained expectations but belongs
to the unnormalized weight. Rescaling the two reference volumes by
#{a_X,a_Y>0} multiplies #{c_C} by #{a_Y}, #{Z_S} by #{a_X} and
#{Z_Q} by #{a_Xa_Y}; normalized laws remain unchanged.
For #{Y=0}, #{c_C=1}; for #{X=0}, #{Z_S=1}. In each case the
unique zero-space endomorphism is invertible and has determinant one.}
\p{Square completion is supported by
\citet{Appendix A.5.5, pp. 650-651}{boyd2004convex}, and Gaussian
normalization by
\citet{Appendix A.2, equation (A.4), p. 200}{rasmussen2006gaussian}.
The invariant integration and determinant argument above reconstruct
these ingredients with specified pairings and volume.
The orthonormal and counting-coordinate calculation in \ref{fdip-00JY}
gives the pivot, covariance and formalization conventions explicitly.
The carrier, split, input precision and volumes are supplied;
#{(S,c_C)} is induced relative to those choices.
The [singular-pivot boundary](fdip-0039) requires a separate argument.}
\p{The operation is algebraic: it acts on vertex and edge sets that are
supplied, and carries neither a dynamical carrier nor an effective metric,
which are additional data.}

\p{For these real data, \ref{fdip-00K8} identifies the embedded
endomorphism #{i_XSp_X:E\to E} as the
\vocabk{shorted operator}{fdip-00K6} of #{Q} along #{i_X(X)}.
Its quadratic value is the minimum over the eliminated field.
The fiber integral above additionally supplies the raw scalar #{c_C}
relative to the specified volume.}

\p{Remark. The identity #{\det Q=\det C\det S} is the factorization
of the action on the top exterior power of #{X\oplus Y}. The same
reading of an eliminated Grassmann block is the remark in
\ref{fdip-009J}.}
